            "summary": [
              {
                "type": "summary_text",
                "text": "**Exploring cooking methods**\n\nI'm thinking about answering generically about cooking techniques. The best method often hinges on the cut and equipment. For thick steaks, the reverse sear is a winner, and for thinner cuts, pan-searing works well. It's crucial to share helpful temperature guidelines and the impact of dry brining and resting. USDA recommends 145°F for safety, but culinary preferences vary. For a 1.5-inch ribeye, the reverse sear can start by baking to about 110-115°F then searing to finish at the desired temp."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "msg_02ace6e5f44baad8006a96ead6984c87d2a950b0e694cb6b07",
            "type": "message",
            "status": "completed",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "annotations": [],
                "logprobs": [],
                "text": "For a **thick steak (1½–2 inches), reverse-searing** is the most reliable way to get an evenly cooked center and a great crust.\n\n### Reverse-sear method\n1. **Salt ahead:** Season generously with kosher salt 1–24 hours before cooking. Refrigerate uncovered if possible.\n2. **Dry and season:** Pat very dry; add black pepper.\n3. **Low oven:** Place on a wire rack over a sheet pan at **250°F / 120°C**. Cook until the center is about **10–15°F below your target**—roughly **115–120°F for medium-rare**.\n4. **Sear:** Heat a cast-iron or heavy pan until extremely hot. Add a thin layer of high-smoke-point oil, then sear the steak **45–60 seconds per side**, including the edges.\n5. **Finish:** During the final 30 seconds, optionally add butter, garlic, and thyme and baste.\n6. **Rest briefly:** Let it sit for about **5 minutes**, then slice across the grain.\n\n### Final temperature guide\n- Rare: **120–125°F / 49–52°C**\n- Medium-rare: **130–135°F / 54–57°C**\n- Medium: **140–145°F / 60–63°C**\n\nUse an instant-read thermometer—temperature is much more reliable than timing. For steaks under about **1 inch thick**, skip the oven and simply sear in a very hot pan for 2–3 minutes per side."
              }
